<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
<p class="font-bold">Game B looks at how much money you have, so playing A first can change B’s chances.</p>
<div class="grid gap-3 text-base sm:grid-cols-3"><div class="border-2 border-ink p-3"><strong>Game A</strong><p>49.5% chance of winning £1.</p><p>Each play shifts your money.</p></div><div class="border-2 border-ink p-3"><strong>Money now?</strong><p>Is it a multiple of three?</p><p>Your previous plays decide.</p></div><div class="border-2 border-ink p-3"><strong>Game B</strong><p>Multiple of 3: 9.5% chance to win.</p><p>Otherwise: 74.5%.</p></div></div>
<div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-base"><div class="border-l-4 border-[#387a91] pl-3"><p class="font-bold">AABB AABB …</p><p>Winning on average</p></div><div class="border-l-4 border-[#bd4b37] pl-3"><p class="font-bold">ABAB ABAB …</p><p>Losing on average</p></div></div>
<figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">Neither game wins by itself in the stated example. But Game A changes which version of Game B is played next. The pattern of play changes the chances, so “two losing games” is not the same as independent losing bets.</figcaption></figure>